##
## Amphiphile bilayer demo parameter file.
##
## A single 5-bead amphiphile HHTTT: two hydrophilic HEAD beads (H) and three
## hydrophobic TAIL beads (T). The interactions encode the two ingredients of the
## hydrophobic effect that drive bilayer (membrane) formation:
##
##   * TAILS attract each other and hide from solvent - T-T is strongly attractive
##     (-20) and each T-solvent contact is penalised (+4). Tails therefore bury
##     themselves into a hydrophobic core, away from the empty "solvent" sites.
##   * HEADS like solvent - each H-solvent contact is favourable (-4), so the heads
##     sit on the two outer surfaces of the membrane facing the solvent.
##   * HEAD/TAIL demixing - H-T is mildly repulsive (+6), keeping heads and tails in
##     their separate layers rather than intermixing.
##
## Together these make a tails-in / heads-out BILAYER a stable arrangement.
##
## Bead types:  H = hydrophilic head,  T = hydrophobic tail
##

## ANGLE PENALTIES (fully flexible chains)
ANGLE_PENALTY	H	0	0	0
ANGLE_PENALTY	T	0	0	0

## PAIRWISE SHORT-RANGE INTERACTIONS (negative = attractive, positive = repulsive)
H	H	0
H	T	6
T	T	-20

## bead-solvent interactions ("<type> 0 <energy>"): heads favour solvent (-4),
## tails are penalised for touching it (+4) - i.e. tails are hydrophobic.
H	0	-4
T	0	4
